3|0@MapPorn^1440489708#cuev8rl:seszett$:Well, that's just what colonising means. Now if you would like a map of previously inhabited colonies, that might be interesting too but it would be different, and more difficult as well: Brazil counts as inhabited? When France colonised the Rio de Janeiro area though (it didn't last long before the Portuguese destroyed the colony) they chose an uninhabited place, so does it count as inhabited because Brazil as a whole was inhabited or uninhabited because the area was not? What about the numerous Caribbean islands which were first depopulated by the Spanish, and later settled by France or Britain when they were uninhabited? I think it's a lot more complicated than just settling inhabited or uninhabited places, and especially when dealing with vast territories like Brazil, Russia or Canada.
