1|0@MapPorn^1440495484#cuewk31:Nimonic$:To be fair, we also settled large parts of Scotland and Ireland. There were definitely people there before. I wouldn't say we were ever a colony of Denmark and Sweden. Denmark at a stretch, for a short period, but not Sweden. 
0|0@MapPorn^1440496189#cuewpqd:nod23$:&gt; To be fair, we also settled large parts of Scotland and Ireland. That's a good point, we did kill off some Picts in Scotland. However, we didn't settle it and stay. In fact that's why the Celts took over Scotland. In Ireland we established Dublin, but we were eventually kicked out. I'm not sure it counts as a colony [in this context]? &gt; I wouldn't say we were ever a colony of Denmark and Sweden. Denmark at a stretch, for a short period, but not Sweden. Firstly, we were made a *province* of Denmark [for a long period]. Secondly, the King of *sovereign* Norway was forced to abdicate in 1814 to allow the Swedish King his spoils of war. The fact that we lost our sovereignty, were ruled from Stockholm, and had no foreign policy of our own are arguments that weigh heavily in my favor. [Even our flag was no longer our own!](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UTduy7Qkvk8)
1|0@MapPorn^1440496535#cuewsk4:Nimonic$:&gt;Firstly, we were made a province of Denmark. Yes, that is the relatively short period I'm allowing where we might be considered a colony. &gt;Secondly, the King of sovereign Norway was forced to abdicate in 1814 to allow the Swedish King his spoils of war. The fact that we lost our sovereignty, were ruled from Stockholm, and had no foreign policy of our own are arguments that weigh heavily in my favor. Even our flag was no longer our own! I'm not sure what point you're making here. We were certainly a lot more independent from 1814-1905 than we ever were under the Danes. Besides, this all again depends on how you define a colony. Not every territory that is ever conquered or otherwise acquired by another country is a colony. In fact, most aren't. A colony is generally (but not always) in some way "overseas", without close historical ties or in some other way clearly distinct. We have a shared history with the Danes and the Swedes stretching back over a thousand years. We could become part of Denmark or Sweden, but I don't think we could ever be commonly accepted a *colony*. 
1|0@MapPorn^1440496855#cuewv6q:nod23$:&gt; We were certainly a lot more independent from 1814-1905 than we ever were under the Danes. Yes, more independent, not unlike British Dominions. &gt; I don't think we could ever be commonly accepted a colony. colony; a country or area under the full or *partial* political control of another country and occupied by settlers from that country. The Danish nobility and landowners qualify in my opinion. The Swedes didn't move in, but they did hold powerful positions. I suppose the best name for our relationship is feudal (vassal), but I agree with you that this post is rather misleading since it doesn't define what a "colony" is :)
2|0@MapPorn^1440497415#cuewzul:Nimonic$:&gt;I suppose the best name for our relationship is feudal (vassal), but I agree with you that this post is rather misleading since it doesn't define what a "colony" is :) Indeed, The rules of what makes a "colony" are very fuzzy when it comes to the feudal era, and our later relationship with Denmark very much grew out of such a situation. The aspect of personal unions muddies the waters even further. Except for that mentioned period as a Danish province, Norway was always considered a separate kingdom before, during and after our... let's call it subjugation. I guess we could look at India and its separate title, "Empress of India", but I'd argue the difference lies in the fact that said title was created *by* the colonizers. I suppose it could be argued either way! In any case it is as you say, it's very hard to reach a consensus when we don't even really know what we mean when we discuss the word "colony". Suffice it to say we're on top now. Until the oil (fund) runs out, anyway. 
